<section class="pathway-section" id="education">

<div class="pathway-header">

<div class="pathway-number">01</div>

<div>
<h2>Traditional Education Intelligence</h2>

<p>
Do not think of college as simply "pay tuition → attend classes
→ receive degree." Institutions contain multiple financial,
academic, professional, and informational systems.
Learn how to navigate the system.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="lesson">

<div class="lesson-label">The overlooked strategy</div>

<h3>The institution itself is a resource network.</h3>

<p>
Students often search only for scholarships. But universities
also contain departmental awards, research opportunities,
faculty relationships, tutoring, career centers, alumni networks,
student employment, competitions, professional organizations,
transfer agreements, and specialized programs.
</p>

</div>


<div class="resource-cards">

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🏛 Department-Level Money</h4>
<p>
Look beyond university-wide scholarships. Departments may
offer awards, travel funding, research support, conference
funding, and discipline-specific opportunities.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🔬 Faculty Research</h4>
<p>
Professors sometimes need research assistants, project support,
data collection, literature reviews, technical assistance,
or administrative help.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🎤 Academic Competitions</h4>
<p>
Case competitions, writing contests, innovation challenges,
business-plan competitions, hackathons, and research showcases
can create evidence and connections.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🤝 Alumni Intelligence</h4>
<p>
Alumni can provide information about industries, hiring patterns,
internships, skills, companies, and career paths that are not
obvious from job descriptions.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧭 Transfer Agreements</h4>
<p>
Community colleges and universities may have transfer pathways
that reduce cost, simplify admission, or provide structured
progression into bachelor's programs.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📚 Academic Support</h4>
<p>
Tutoring, writing centers, career advising, library research
support, accessibility services, and academic workshops can
increase performance without requiring private services.
</p>
</div>

</div>

<br>

<div class="two-column">

<div class="resource-panel">

<h3>Questions Most Applicants Never Ask</h3>

<ul>
<li>Which departments have their own funding?</li>
<li>What research projects need assistants?</li>
<li>Which courses have unusually strong employer connections?</li>
<li>What competitions can produce public evidence?</li>
<li>Which alumni work in my target industry?</li>
<li>What campus jobs build transferable skills?</li>
<li>Are there accelerated or transfer pathways?</li>
<li>What professional associations does the school fund?</li>
</ul>

</div>

<div class="resource-panel">

<h3>Institutional Leverage Map</h3>

<ul>
<li>Financial aid office → funding intelligence</li>
<li>Academic department → specialized opportunities</li>
<li>Career center → employer intelligence</li>
<li>Faculty → research and mentorship</li>
<li>Alumni office → relationship access</li>
<li>Library → information infrastructure</li>
<li>Student organizations → leadership evidence</li>
<li>Career fairs → market feedback</li>
</ul>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- ======================================================
     PATH 2
====================================================== -->

<section class="pathway-section" id="selfeducation">

<div class="pathway-header">

<div class="pathway-number">02</div>

<div>
<h2>Self-Education Intelligence</h2>

<p>
The problem with self-education is rarely access to information.
The problem is converting information into credible evidence.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="lesson">

<div class="lesson-label">Higher-Level Lesson</div>

<h3>Learning has economic value only when it changes what you can demonstrate.</h3>

<p>
Completing a course is evidence that you completed a course.
A functioning project demonstrates that you can apply knowledge.
A case study demonstrates reasoning.
A measurable result demonstrates impact.
The strongest self-education system moves from
<strong>consumption → application → evidence → visibility.</strong>
</p>

</div>


<div class="resource-cards">

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧪 Build Before You Feel Ready</h4>
<p>
Create small projects while learning. Waiting until the end
of a course delays evidence production.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📁 Create a Proof Portfolio</h4>
<p>
Keep screenshots, explanations, before-and-after results,
decisions made, problems encountered, and lessons learned.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📊 Measure Your Projects</h4>
<p>
Track time saved, errors reduced, conversion improved,
processes simplified, pages created, users helped, or
other meaningful outcomes.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧠 Reconstruct Real Problems</h4>
<p>
Take public business problems and create your own analysis,
dashboard, website, marketing plan, workflow, or solution.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🔎 Reverse Engineer Job Descriptions</h4>
<p>
Extract recurring skills from job postings and build projects
specifically designed to demonstrate those skills.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🌐 Publish Learning Artifacts</h4>
<p>
Tutorials, case studies, GitHub projects, reports, dashboards,
and demonstrations make invisible learning visible.
</p>
</div>

</div>


