<div class="lesson">

<div class="lesson-label">Analysis</div>

<h3>Why the final two levels matter.</h3>

<p>
Employers cannot directly observe everything you learned.
They need signals. A case study gives them something to inspect.
A measurable result gives them something to evaluate.
This is why two people can complete the same course but have
very different career outcomes.
</p>

</div>

</section>


<!-- ======================================================
     PATH 3
====================================================== -->

<section class="pathway-section" id="career">

<div class="pathway-header">

<div class="pathway-number">03</div>

<div>
<h2>Career Advancement Intelligence</h2>

<p>
Your current job may contain more career capital than you realize.
The goal is to identify the systems, responsibilities,
metrics, and problems hidden inside the work you already perform.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="lesson">

<div class="lesson-label">Career Translation Principle</div>

<h3>Do not describe only what you did. Translate what the work proves.</h3>

<p>
"I worked inventory" is a task description.
"I maintained inventory accuracy and identified discrepancies"
is a capability description.
"I reduced recurring inventory errors by identifying the source
of discrepancies" becomes an impact statement.
</p>

</div>


<div class="resource-cards">

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📈 Build an Achievement Ledger</h4>
<p>
Record problems solved, improvements made, customer issues
resolved, responsibilities expanded, and situations where
you prevented mistakes.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🔄 Map Transferable Skills</h4>
<p>
Translate customer service, scheduling, inventory, cooking,
operations, sales, or administration into business capabilities.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📋 Study Internal Job Postings</h4>
<p>
Internal openings reveal which skills the organization values.
Use them as a roadmap for what to learn next.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧩 Volunteer for System Problems</h4>
<p>
Recurring operational problems can become opportunities to
demonstrate analytical thinking and initiative.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🗣 Request Better Feedback</h4>
<p>
Instead of asking "How am I doing?", ask which behaviors would
make you more valuable for the next level.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📊 Create a Promotion Evidence File</h4>
<p>
Maintain documentation of accomplishments throughout the year
instead of trying to reconstruct them during a review.
</p>
</div>

</div>


<br>

<div class="decision">

<div class="decision-card">
<strong>Low visibility?</strong>
Create measurable documentation of your contributions.
</div>

<div class="decision-card">
<strong>Low credentials?</strong>
Build projects or certifications aligned with target roles.
</div>

<div class="decision-card">
<strong>Low experience?</strong>
Use current responsibilities to generate transferable evidence.
</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- ======================================================
     PATH 4
====================================================== -->

<section class="pathway-section" id="business">

<div class="pathway-header">

<div class="pathway-number">04</div>

<div>
<h2>Entrepreneurship Intelligence</h2>

<p>
Entrepreneurship does not begin with a logo, website,
business card, or large inventory order.
It begins with evidence that someone has a problem
worth solving.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="lesson">

<div class="lesson-label">Capital-Light Principle</div>

<h3>Use customer information as a form of capital.</h3>

<p>
Before spending money, learn. Interview potential customers.
Study complaints. Analyze competitors. Identify repeated tasks.
Find expensive inefficiencies. Test a small service.
The objective is to reduce uncertainty before increasing spending.
</p>

</div>


<div class="resource-cards">

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🔍 Complaint Mining</h4>
<p>
Study reviews, forums, support questions, app-store complaints,
and public discussions to identify recurring problems.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧪 Service Before Product</h4>
<p>
A service can test demand before you invest in software,
inventory, manufacturing, or a complicated product.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>📞 Customer Interviews</h4>
<p>
Ask potential customers how they currently solve the problem,
what it costs them, and what makes existing solutions frustrating.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🧱 Manual First, Automation Later</h4>
<p>
Perform a process manually before building technology around it.
This exposes what customers actually value.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>🤝 Partnership Leverage</h4>
<p>
Look for organizations that already have the audience you need.
A partnership can substitute for expensive advertising.
</p>
</div>

<div class="resource-card">
<h4>💰 Pre-Sale Testing</h4>
<p>
Where appropriate, validate whether people will commit before
building the full offer.
</p>
</div>

</div>


<br>

<div class="experiment">

<div class="experiment-card">
<small>EXPERIMENT 01</small>
<h4>Problem Interview</h4>
<p>Talk to 10 potential users and identify repeated pain points.</p>
</div>

<div class="experiment-card">
<small>EXPERIMENT 02</small>
<h4>Manual Service</h4>
<p>Deliver the outcome manually before building a system.</p>
</div>

<div class="experiment-card">
<small>EXPERIMENT 03</small>
<h4>Small Offer</h4>
<p>Create a narrow offer designed around one specific problem.</p>
</div>

<div class="experiment-card">
<small>EXPERIMENT 04</small>
<h4>Distribution Test</h4>
<p>Test whether you can reach potential customers consistently.</p>
</div>

</div>

</section>


